cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "RetainIQ: causal retention decisioning for small subscription businesses"
message: "If you use this software or the simulator, please cite the work below."
type: software
version: 2.0.1
date-released: "2026-10-15"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22025879
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/PrashamJ17/PBL-Proj"
authors:
  - given-names: Prasham
    family-names: Jain
    affiliation: "Manipal University Jaipur"
  - given-names: Rishi
    family-names: Gupta
    affiliation: "Manipal University Jaipur"
abstract: >-
  Research software on what a small subscription business can learn from its
  own data about whom to send a retention offer, and whether it can tell
  afterwards that the offer worked. It contains a subscription simulator that
  gives each customer's churn probability with and without an offer; benchmark
  harnesses for three public randomised experiments; an analysis of how often
  targeting methods beat random selection as the training set shrinks; a
  measurement of the correlation between risk and response over repeated
  splits; a Bayesian decision rule that can decline to act, with a
  re-implementation of the rule of Lemmens and Gupta (2020) for comparison; a
  holdout module with minimum-detectable-effect calculations; and a
  discrete-time competing-risks survival model.
keywords:
  - uplift modelling
  - heterogeneous treatment effects
  - causal inference
  - customer retention
  - randomised experiments
  - small samples
  - statistical power
  - survival analysis
  - subscription business
